# ── Build output / IDE ────────────────────────────────────────────────
[Bb]in/
[Oo]bj/
.vs/
*.user
*.suo
*.rsuser

# Claude Code local session data
.claude/

# ── Local machine paths ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# Where this machine keeps the game's assemblies and its BepInEx folder.
# Directory.Build.props.example is the tracked template.
Directory.Build.props

# ── Local migration workspace ─────────────────────────────────────────
# ModForge is a generic toolkit. A specific mod's pack, and whatever is used
# to migrate its content across, stays on the machine doing the migrating --
# none of it is needed for the editor or the PackPlugin to build or run.
# Per-mod names for these live in .git/info/exclude, not here.
modpack BAK.json
# Tools/ used to be ignored wholesale. That took the build's own scripts with
# it: MakeArtThumbnails.py regenerates the art a release ships, the
# Tools/UnityEditor extractors are the only way to refresh the vanilla
# catalogs, and none of it existed anywhere but one machine. Tracked now.
__pycache__/
*.pyc
# One-off conversion / shader-analysis scratch scripts
_convert_*.py
_port_scenes.py
_render.py
extract_shader*.py
disasm_shader.py
shader_dump/

# Local scratch packs / manifest backups
# The practice art ships with the editor from SMSModForge/Resources/TutorialAssets/,
# tracked, because a tutorial whose sprites exist on one machine is no tutorial.
# Belt-and-braces: never track a manifest from that folder, whatever a test run
# happens to write into it.
SMSModForge/Resources/TutorialAssets/**/modpack.json
modpack - Copy.json

# Test project build output
SMSModForge.Tests/bin/
SMSModForge.Tests/obj/

# ── The vanilla UI extraction ────────────────────────────────────────
#
# About 100 MB: the per-surface trees, every distinct sprite crop, and the
# TextMeshPro font atlases and the extracted UI art. The editor SHIPS these —
# they are what makes the UI preview show the game rather than an impression of
# it — and they are TRACKED now. That was an open question and it is settled:
# leaving them out cost a fresh clone the UI preview, which is half of what the
# UI tab is for, and a release could only be built on the one machine that had
# run the extraction.
#
# The cost is known and accepted: about 100 MB in every clone, and a
# re-extraction ADDS another 100 MB rather than replacing it, because git keeps
# both. If that becomes a problem the answer is Git LFS, not un-tracking them
# again.
#
# report.txt and fonts.json stay out — they are extraction diagnostics, not
# something the build reads.
#
# To regenerate, in the game's Unity project with the Game view at 1920x1080:
#   Tools > SMSModForge > Extract Vanilla UI Surfaces (resolve dormant)
#   Tools > SMSModForge > Export TMP Font Assets
# writing both into SMSModForge/Resources/VanillaOverlays/, then
#   python Tools/Ui/BuildUiCatalog.py SMSModForge/Resources/VanillaOverlays \
#          -o SMSModForge/Resources/VanillaUi/vanilla_ui_bases.json
# The catalog IS tracked — it is 79 KB and it is what the tab needs to list
# what a pack can extend, so the tab works on a fresh clone even unpreviewed.
SMSModForge/Resources/VanillaOverlays/report.txt
SMSModForge/Resources/VanillaOverlays/fonts.json
